{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:BraidsAndKnots.HoefsmitToroidalLadderLocus","@type":"LeanModule","name":"BraidsAndKnots.HoefsmitToroidalLadderLocus","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.GroupWithZero.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.LinearCombination"],"declarations":2,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.toroidal_locus_forced","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.toroidal_locus_forced","kind":"theorem","line":61,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/hoefsmit-toroidal-ladder-locus.lean#L61-L78","doc":"**Uniqueness (the forcing theorem).** If the ladder factor at constant\nshifts `(α, β)` reproduces `d² - 1` identically in `d`, then\n`α = -1/2` and `β ∈ {1/2, -3/2}` (the Legendre reflection pair\n`μ ↔ -μ - 1`), stated division-free as `2α = -1` and\n`2β ∈ {1, -3}`. The half-integer degree shift is forced.\n\n`[IsDomain R]` is load-bearing — see the module note for the\nproduct-ring counterexample that breaks this over a bare `CommRing`. ","formalizes":[{"@id":"block:prop:hoefsmit-toroidal-ladder-locus","label":"prop:hoefsmit-toroidal-ladder-locus","md":"quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/hoefsmit-toroidal-ladder-locus.md"}],"signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.BraidsAndKnots.toroidal_locus_valid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.BraidsAndKnots.toroidal_locus_valid","kind":"theorem","line":44,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/braids-and-knots/hoefsmit-toroidal-ladder-locus.lean#L44-L59","doc":"The toroidal locus is valid: at α = -1/2, β = 1/2 — division-free,\n`2α = -1` and `2β = 1` — the ladder factor reproduces `d² - 1` for\nevery `d`.  No `IsDomain` and no `Invertible 2` is needed: the\nhypothesis `2 * α = -1` already exhibits `-α` as an inverse of `2`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}